{"title":"2016 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 8","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Gaby lists the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/5/67595d980abc75b2ef17804f0156ddb7e79a9fc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, \\text{and } 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"146\" height=\"17\" />. In her list, the ratio of the number of prime numbers to the number of composite numbers is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/f/2ff824f25001f9c2f1217ac461db710c03f16cba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 3:4 \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ 5:2 \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ 2:5 \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ 3:7 \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ 1:6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"472\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_8&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First, we can list all of the numbers that are prime and then list all of the numbers are composite.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/2/f52f1bde73259b0255b84fca2d27af6e5f6a60fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3, 5, 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> are the prime numbers\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/c/64c8cbc02382a70d36b3afcca0acbe8abeeb77c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4, 6, 8, 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"60\" height=\"16\" /> are the composite numbers\n</p><p>Next, we can count how many numbers are prime and how many are composite. We see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are prime, while <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> are composite.\n</p><p>Using this, the ratio of the number of prime numbers in the list to the number of composite numbers in the list is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/5/435a897821afc9a8581d4a312db49ed132285542.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(A) } 3:4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"82\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)\" title=\"2016 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)\">2016 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2016 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2016 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2016_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2016 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 7\">Pro